    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The historic Golden Gate hotel, originally the Hotel Nevada opened in 1906, still shows traces of its early 20th‑century architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lasvegasthenandnow.com/historic-things-to-see-on-fremont-street/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "El Cortez, the only casino on the National Register of Historic Places, opened in 1910 and still hosts classic neon signage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lasvegasthenandnow.com/historic-things-to-see-on-fremont-street/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Neon Museum on Fremont Street preserves over 2.25 acres of restored Las Vegas casino signs, a living gallery of the city’s lighting history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lasvegasthenandnow.com/historic-things-to-see-on-fremont-street/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1987, U2 filmed the music video for “I Still Haven’t Found What I’m Looking For” right on Fremont Street, with The Edge playing acoustic guitar.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fremont_Street"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Fremont Street earned the nickname “Glitter Gulch” in the 1930s, drawing mobsters, Rat Pack icons, and Hollywood stars to its bustling casinos.",
